lesson: 66
title: How many more to have as many
stars: 0
explain: To have as many as someone else, the one with fewer needs the difference.
explain: Take the smaller amount away from the bigger amount.
explain: Tom has 12 cards and Lily has 8. Lily needs 12 - 8 = 4 more cards to have as many as Tom.
vars: a=name b=name t=thing n=5..60 m=2..55
need: m < n
answer: n - m
exercise: {a} has {n} {t}. {b} has {m} {t}. How many more {t} does {b} need to have as many as {a}?
exercise: {b} has {m} {t} and {a} has {n} {t}. How many more {t} must {b} get to match {a}?
exercise: There are {n} children and {m} chairs. How many more chairs are needed so every child has one?
exercise: {a} picked {n} apples and {b} picked {m}. How many more does {b} have to pick to catch up with {a}?
exercise: The red team has {n} points and the blue team has {m} points. How many points does the blue team need to tie?
test: {b} owns only {m} {t}, while {a} owns {n}. How many {t} would {b} have to add to own the same number?
test: There are {m} cups for {n} people. How many cups are missing if everyone needs one?
test: {a}'s tower has {n} blocks and {b}'s has {m}. How many blocks should {b} add to make the towers equally tall?
exercise: {a} has {n} {t} and {b} has {m}. How many more {t} does {b} need so that they have the same number?
exercise: How many {t} must {b} get to have as many as {a}? {a} has {n} {t} and {b} has {m} {t}.
exercise: In a school in Georgia there are {n} pupils and {m} lunch trays. How many more trays are needed so that each pupil has one?
exercise: {b} has {m} {t}; {a} has {n}. How many {t} is {b} short of {a}'s number?
exercise: A team from Manchester has scored {n} goals this season and a team from Leeds has scored {m}. How many more goals does the Leeds team need to draw level?
exercise: {a} has {n.words} {t}. {b} has {m.words} {t}. How many more must {b} collect to have just as many as {a}?
exercise: There are {n} horses on a ranch in Montana and only {m} saddles. How many more saddles are needed so that every horse has one?
exercise: {b} has saved {m} dollars and {a} has saved {n} dollars. How many more dollars must {b} save to have as much as {a}?
test: In Adelaide, {n} swimmers turn up but there are just {m} towels. How many more towels are needed for one each?
test: {b} wants as many {t} as {a}. {a} has {n}; {b} has {m}. How many {t} is {b} missing?
test: A bakery in Christchurch has {n} cakes but only {m} boxes. How many more boxes does it need to put each cake in its own box?
test: How many {t} would make {b}'s {m} {t} equal to {a}'s {n}?
test: In Belfast, the first jar holds {n} {t} and the second holds {m}. How many {t} must go into the second jar for the jars to hold equal numbers?
test: Equal shares: {a} owns {n} {t}, {b} owns {m}. What must {b} gain to be level?
exercise: {a}'s got {n} {t}, {b} only {m}. how many more {b} need to be even
exercise: {n} kids, {m} chairs. how many more chairs we need
exercise: {b} has {m+1} — no wait, {m} {t} — {a} has {n}. how many more for {b} to match
exercise: {a} {n} {t}, {b} {m}. Kai says {b} needs {n+m} more to match. nah, how many really
test: {m} cups out, {n} people coming for tea, oh and its been raining like 3 days now. how many more cups
test: {a} {n}, {b} {m} {t}. how many {t} {b} gotta get to have as many
test: {n} {t} vs {m} {t}, how many more does the smaller pile need to be the same
exam: Whereas {a} holds {n} {t} and {b} holds {m}, determine the number of {t} which {b} must additionally acquire in order that the two holdings be equal.
exam: Ascertain the quantity by which a holding of {m} {t} falls short of a holding of {n} {t}.
exam: It is given that {b} possesses {m} {t}. State the number of further {t} required for {b}'s holding to equal that of {a}, namely {n}.
exam: Compute the additional quantity of {t} to be furnished to {b}, who holds {m.words}, such that parity with {a}, who holds {n.words}, is attained.
exercise: {a}'s family had {n} dollars a week before Dad got let go. Now they have {m}. How many more dollars a week do they need to have as much as before?
exercise: {b}'s teacher calls {b} 'a reluctant reader'. {b} has read {m} books this year and {a} has read {n}. How many more books must {b} read to catch up with {a}?
test: oi {b}, quit 'bending the truth' — {a}'s got {n} {t}, you've got {m}, not more. how many short are ya
